  "body": "## What happens\n\nThe generic component editor redraws a holder after a gauge-length edit in `component_edit.rs::build`. It looks up the library entry with `holder_entry` (`crates/ui/src/egui_app/state_components.rs`, about line 377). That function matches the entry by `library_ref`, id or name, not by the outline.\n\nA user holder that carries a library name (for example a copy of \"BT40 Shrink 20 mm\") but has a wider body than the library entry (for example a body that the user drew or imported, hidden under the flange envelope) gets the library body at the new gauge length. The new outline can then be smaller than the real steel. Holder outlines go into the collision checks, so a check can pass when the real holder collides.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Found by the independent pre-review of PR #4469 (fix for #4455) at head `1e899ad`. The route is outside the diff of #4469 and older than it.\n- #4469 closes the same class of defect for the holder form and the MCP tool `edit_user_holder`: both now write through `HolderShape::component`, which refuses a shape that does not read back as itself.\n\n## Expected\n\nA gauge-length edit never draws a holder outline smaller than the steel that the holder had before the edit. Options:\n- build the new outline from the holder's own outline (stretch or shorten the shank part) and never from a library entry found by name; or\n- use the library entry only when the holder's outline equals the library entry's outline at its library gauge length, and refuse otherwise with a named reason.\n\n## Done means\n\n- A test: a user holder with a library name and a wider hidden body gets a gauge-length edit in the generic editor; the new outline is never smaller than the old outline at any height, or the edit is refused by name and nothing changes.\n- The existing copies of library holders still edit as before when their outline equals the library outline.\n- No change to toolpaths, posts or the project file format.\n",
